# Board

## Summary
A kanban over any cards, consuming `KanbanPlane` from boxel-ui: the drag engine, columns and WIP limits come free, so consume, don't rebuild. The block adds the card-level contract: `@items` (CardDef[]), `@columns` (`{ key, label?, color? }[]`), `@columnKeyFor(item)` mapping a card to its column, and `@onMove(item, columnKey)` fired when a drag crosses columns. The consumer persists the result, typically by setting a status or stage field and saving.

## API
- `@onOpen(item)` fires on a tap, `@onSelect(item)` on pointer-down and keyboard navigation, `@onAddCard(columnKey)` renders a per-column add affordance the plane draws itself. `KanbanPlane` already tells a click apart from a drag; these hand that through in card terms so a consumer never reasons about placements.
- A tap both selects and opens. That is usable rather than conflicting because the board stays on screen when a card opens, so consumer actions belong beside the board keyed to the selection, not on the card face, which is the drag handle.
- Cards render in their own fitted format by default; the plane supplies the sized container, so wrapping cards again produces dead space. Pass `<:card as |item|>` to customise. Column `color` tints the header, which is how a consumer expresses stage semantics without forking the block.
- `@hideEmpty` collapses columns with no cards.

## Usage
```gts
import { Board, type BoardColumn } from '@cardstack/catalog/components/board';

columns: BoardColumn[] = StatusField.statusOptions.map((o) => ({ key: o.value, label: o.label ?? o.value }));
columnKeyFor = (item: CardDef) => (item as Task).status;
onMove = (item: CardDef, key: string) => {
  if (canTransition(StatusField, (item as Task).status, key)) (item as Task).status = key;
};

<Board @boardLabel='Tasks' @items={{this.tasks}} @columns={{this.columns}} @columnKeyFor={{this.columnKeyFor}} @onMove={{this.onMove}} />
```
The linked example mounts this board and the Table over the same task records, with columns taken from the Status field's option set and moves gated by its transition graph.

## Non-goals
Persisting column order or per-card sort order (in-memory only). Query-driven item loading (the consumer's job). Interactive controls inside cards: the drag engine claims `pointerdown`, so a child that needs its own clicks must stop propagation.
